			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4408537">
									

<p>Why have both standard and ACS classifications? The only reason I can see is to try and please everyone, but that misses the point of design elements - both standard and ACS classifications should be striving for simplicity and stripping unnecessary information from the point of view of being aesthetically pleasing as well as a smoother narrative experience, so having both is strictly worse.</p>
<p>I don't think decommissioned as a class adds anything beyond what is already on offer with neutralized. The class reflects the current state of the object (re: containment) - if you introduce 'how did this object get into this state' as part of the criteria this is fluff none of the other classes have. The decom form also looks like more of a meme template rather than something a real organisation would actually use - for an action which is presumably rarely done and only in extreme circumstances, pro-forma checkboxes with generic and poorly-detailed answers are not fit for purpose.</p>

							</div>

								
